  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA very much welcomed period of high pressure and quiet weather is in \nstore for the region over the next 2 days. An upper level ridge over \nthe southern US will build to the north and allow a surface high to \nslide into the area from the WNW. Ahead of the arrival of the \nstronger portion of this high, an upper level disturbance will \nripple through the area and allow for some isolated showers with a \nrumble of thunder possible across portions of the area along and \neast of I-81 this afternoon. The full force of high pressure will \nsettle in during the evening and sit overhead through Saturday. NNW \nflow from this pattern will keep temperatures near normal with \npartly to mostly clear skies across the region. Temps Friday night \nwill be the coolest we have seen in a while, with most seeing lows \nin the low to mid 50s. A few 40s will be possible across N\nOneida county and in the Catskills. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nOn Sunday, the ridge will break down as an upper level trough moves \ninto the area from the Great Lakes. A surface low will develop and \ntrack over the area, bringing scattered showers and isolated \nthunderstorms to the region Sunday morning into the overnight hours. \nThe current forecasted track of the storm keeps much of the \ninstability to our south while 0-6km bulk shear associated with the \nlow pressure system is in the 35-45kt range. With the lack of \ninstability, severe storms are not expected at this time. PWATs\nwill increase to around 2in, pointing to a chance for heavy rain\nfall, especially where we might be able to get some convection\ngoing, that could lead to isolated flash flooding. This would be\ndependent on where the heaviest rain fell as portions of the\narea are more susceptible to flash flooding given the multiple\nrounds of rain we have seen over portions of the area during the\npast week. We will have to monitor the evolution of this system\nas a track of the low a little farther to the north will allow \nsurface based CAPE in the 2000-3000 j/kg range to work its way \nin to the region. These numbers combined with the aforementioned\nshear values would allow for widespread severe weather chances \nacross the region. A more northerly track would also bring\nbetter heavy rain chances and thus better chances for flash \nflooding. A Marginal Risk for excessive rainfall leading to\nflash flooding is currently in place across the CWA for Sunday\nwith a Slight risk just to our south. This slight could move\nnorth if the track of the low also moves north.\n\nBehind this strong low pressure system, another ridge with \nassociated surface high will build into the area by Tuesday morning. \nThis high pressure system should control the weather pattern through \nmid-week. Another trough moving in from the Great Lakes will be \npossible to close out the work week. \n\nTemperatures next week will be around normal to slightly below \nnormal given the mostly consistent WNW flow pushing a Canadian \nairmass into the region.",
